From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Leo Lobato <leo@lobato.org>
Date: Wed, 28 Oct 2026 18:10:50 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] PC5: Wine's own heap is kept in direct memory, with headroom
 for the game

prospero-win 22f0d48 (#470), after the v0.2.0 PC5 builds on, picked onto
PC5's patches. PC5's memory line keeps its runs_* counters; the heap's
two follow them as counters 11 and 12.

pw_wine_heap took its 1 MiB spans and large blocks from anonymous
mappings, which a title draws from its ~440 MiB of flexible memory; patch
0600 had moved the game's own memory to direct memory, but not this heap,
which the whole Unix side (Mesa included) allocates from. OpenGL games
used flexible memory up: Slay the Spire 2 reached flexible_free=0 with
276 MiB of heap, then ran at 4 fps, took null-page faults and exited.

The heap's backing is now pluggable (pw_wine_heap_set_backing): map may
refuse, and the heap falls back to an anonymous mapping; unmap reports
whether it owned the range, so blocks mapped before the backing was set
are still unmapped as before. Once direct memory passes its self-check,
pw_wine_dmem_ps5.c installs a backing that allocates main direct memory,
maps it where the kernel chooses and keeps its offset so freeing a large
block releases it. WINE_PS5_HEAP_FLEXIBLE keeps the old behaviour.

The heap leaves the game headroom: the pool is shared with ntdll's
committed memory and the GPU driver, and Over the Hill at 4K filled all
12 GiB. When the largest free block of direct memory would drop below
256 MiB, a new span or large block is an anonymous mapping of flexible
memory instead, up to 128 MiB of them; past that the heap takes direct
memory again (WINE_PS5_HEAP_HEADROOM_MB, WINE_PS5_HEAP_SPILL_MB). Free
direct memory is a running estimate of what the heap took and gave back,
read from the kernel again after 16 MiB of net growth, so allocating and
freeing adds no kernel call. The table of direct blocks, 2^16 slots, is
itself direct memory taken when the backing is installed, so ntdll.prx's
.bss does not grow. The heap's direct and spilled bytes are memory
counters 8 and 9, logged as heap_dmem and heap_spill.

On a PS5 (2026-10-28), with only ntdll.prx swapped, Half-Life 2's main
menu and OpenArena with two bots ran at the same frame rate and process
CPU time as before, with 26 and 5 MiB more flexible memory free and no
direct-memory failures.

Co-authored-by: Manuel Pereira <manuelpereiralds@gmail.com>
Co-authored-by: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
